// Language section: Schema & Decorators.
page schema { sites = [:reference, :wcl_skill]  title = "Schema & Decorators"
  h1 "Schema & Decorators"

  p "Decorators (written `@name` or `@name(args)`) attach schema metadata to declarations and fields. Together they describe a document's legal structure, which `wcl check` validates."

  h2 "Declaration decorators"

  table {
    rows:
      | "Decorator"          | "On"   | "Meaning" |
      | "`@document`"        | "type" | "Marks the document root schema (composes per namespace — see below)" |
      | "`@block(\"kind\")`" | "type" | "Makes the type a nestable block of that kind" |
      | "`@table(\"kind\")`" | "type" | "Row schema for pipe-table syntax" |
      | "`@decorator(\"name\")`" | "type" | "Makes the type the schema for a user-defined `@name` decorator — its fields type the decorator's arguments" |
      | "`@schemaless`"      | "type" | "Opens the type: every instance accepts undeclared fields / children (no membership check), as if each were marked `@schemaless`. Also used on the document/reflection path." |
      | "`@by_ref`"          | "type" | "When a block of this kind sits in a `@child`/`@children` slot of a record read as data, the slot reifies to a resolvable reference (a path) instead of inlining the block — so the content can be carried on the record and rendered elsewhere (e.g. wdoc's `body` + `project`)" |
      | "`@dynamic`"         | "connection" | "Lets `->` operands name ids resolved at consume time, not just literal blocks (see Connections)" |
  }

  h2 "Field decorators"

  table {
    rows:
      | "Decorator"            | "Meaning" |
      | "`@child(\"kind\")`"   | "A single nested block of the given kind" |
      | "`@children(\"kind\")`" | "A list of nested blocks (or union variants — dispatch)" |
      | "`@inline(slot)`"      | "Bind the block label to a field at that position" |
      | "`@default(expr)`"     | "Default value when the field is omitted" |
      | "`@connections(S)`"    | "Accumulate `->` connection statements as records" |
      | "`@min(n)` / `@max(n)`" | "Numeric range constraint, checked by `wcl check`" |
      | "`@non_empty`"          | "The string / list value must not be empty" |
      | "`@ref(\"kind\")`"      | "The id value(s) must name an existing block of that kind — `wcl check` flags a dangling reference" |
  }

  p "The constraint decorators also attach to a [type alias](records) (`@min(1) type Port = u16`), applying to every field declared with the alias."

  h2 "Referential integrity with @ref"

  p "A field that holds an `identifier` (or `list<identifier>`) which is _semantically a reference_ to another block can declare that with `@ref(\"kind\")`. `wcl check` then verifies every id names an existing block of that kind — anywhere in the document, top-level or nested — and reports a dangling reference otherwise. An unset optional reference is skipped."

  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
@block("screen") type Screen { @inline(0) id: identifier  name: utf8 }
@block("flow") type Flow {
  @inline(0) id: identifier
  @ref("screen") entry_screen: identifier   // must be a declared screen id
  @ref("screen") steps: list<identifier>    // every element checked
}
WCL
  }

  h2 "Child-count constraints on @block"

  p "`@block` accepts two named arguments that constrain a block's nested children: `max_children = N` caps the total nested-block count, and `required_children = [\"kind\", …]` demands at least one child of each listed kind. Both are enforced by `wcl check`."
  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
@block("stage", max_children = 4, required_children = ["step"])
type Stage {
  @inline(0) name: utf8
  @children("step") steps: list<Step>
}
WCL
  }

  h2 "A worked example"

  p "A document root with one kind of child block, whose label becomes an inline field and whose port has a default:"
  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
@block("service")
type Service {
  @inline(0)   name: utf8     // service "web" → name = "web"
  @default(80) port: u32
  region: utf8
}

@document
type Config {
  @children("service") services: list<Service>
}
WCL
  }

  p "Given that schema, this document validates: each `service` block contributes a `Service` to `services`, with `name` taken from the label and `port` defaulting to `80`."
  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
service "web" { region = "us-east-1" }
service "api" { port = 9090u32  region = "eu-west-1" }
WCL
  }

  h2 "Composing document schemas"

  p "Several `@document` schemas can govern the same namespace, and they **merge**: a top-level field or block is legal if **any** of them declares it. This lets you import a library that ships its own `@document` (for instance wdoc's `Site`) and still add your own top-level tags — just declare your own `@document` at the root."

  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
import <wdoc.wcl>            // brings in wdoc's library @document

@document
type ProjectDoc {           // your own root schema — merges with wdoc's
  @children("project_meta") metas: list<ProjectMeta>
}

@block("project_meta")
type ProjectMeta { @inline(0) id: identifier  owner: utf8 }

project_meta info { owner = "Wil" }   // your tag, alongside wdoc pages
page index { text { span "Hello" {} } }
WCL
  }

  p "Imported (library) `@document` schemas merge silently, so importing several modules that each ship one is fine. Only a **second root-authored** `@document` in the same namespace is an error — you get one root schema, which composes with whatever the imports provide."

  p "When you import `<wdoc.wcl>`, wdoc can auto-generate a reference page for the blocks your `@document` declares: `block_reference { type = ProjectDoc }` emits a heading and property table per block. See [Documenting your own schema](wdoc_data_views)."

  callout "Reflection" {
    class = ["info"]
    body  = "decorator_names(T) and decorator_arg(T, name, slot) read decorators back at evaluation time — used by libraries (like wdoc) that dispatch on a block's declared kind."
  }
}
